{{also|Sepsis|sépsis}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
coined by [[w:Hippocrates|Hippocrates]] From {{der|en|grc|σῆψις||putrefaction}}, from {{m|grc|σήπειν||to make rotten}}, from {{m|grc|σήψ||a kind of lizard; also a kind of serpent whose bite was alleged to cause putrefaction}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛpsɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sepsis.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|sepses}}

# {{lb|en|pathology}} A serious medical [[condition]] in which the whole body is inflamed, causing injury to its own tissues and organs as a response to infection.
#* '''1994''', [[w|Martin Amis]], ''New Yorker'', ''reprinted in'' ''The Rub of Time'' (NY: Knopf, 2017), pp. 213-14:
#*: Imagine the '''sepsis''' of helpless loathing [Jimmy Connors] must have inspired in his opponents during his "great runs" at the US Open.

